  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Monday)\nIssued at 1156 AM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nA 596 dam ridge continues to expand and intensify across the Ohio\nValley as of 11 AM. Temperatures are running 1-5 degrees warmer\nthan 24 hours ago with another hot day expected with highs in the\nupper 90s. A Heat Advisory will begin at noon for portions of Mid-\nSouth through 8 PM for heat index values up to 106F. As the\naforementioned ridge retrogrades, moisture will continue to surge\nacross the region. Continued moisture flow combined with stagnant\nupper 90s, and perhaps low 100s, an areawide Heat Advisory will\ngo into effect Wednesday at 11 AM and persist through 8 PM\nSaturday. Limited cooling at night is expected, which makes this\nheat dangerous. The lack of significant nocturnal cooling will\nresult in cumulative heat stress, significantly increasing the\nhealth risk for vulnerable populations.\n\nSimilar to yesterday, diurnally driven showers could develop each\nafternoon providing temporary relief from heat. While isolated\nsummer-time convection is hard to pinpoint, the highest\nprobability for shower development will be along and west of the\nMississippi River.\n\nNo large-scale synoptic changes are expected during the work week\nThere is increased rain chances on Friday evening across north\nMississippi as the remnant moisture from what once was TS Dolly\nand TS Edouard will ride the western periphery of the ridge.\nTroughing will set up in the northeast by Sunday nudging\ndewpoints down slightly, but relief does not appear to be on the\nhorizon. Both the GFS and ECMWF are in good agreement of another\nstrong upper ridge centering over the region early next week.\n\nDNM",
